    "q-161037": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161037",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{__________},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(z,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x=\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}$ by  $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis for $z\\in [1,5].$ What is the second  component of $r?$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14275690/assets/d3302b5f4ea4c0387d70ac8f78e03e486fff9579f1a6b5e5cec08a31cde76af3.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(z)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $z$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(z)=\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}\\ge 0$ for all $z\\in [1,5].$   \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(z,\\theta )=⟨\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,\\,z\\in [1,5],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
