    "q-109773": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109773",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "$$\nr(y,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_______},\\,y,\\,({y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(y,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={y}^{3}-{y}^{4}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [0,1].$ What is the first component of $r?$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14275690/assets/be6fb27c0d050a7007e9f4a0d526daaff40b8f7d01f20c63ac8cc00074121f28.png)\n\n  \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(y)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta =[0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(y)={y}^{3}-{y}^{4}\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,1].$\n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(y,\\theta )=⟨({y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,({y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [0,1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the first component of $r$ is $({y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
