    "q-161069": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161069",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "$$\n⟨\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\text{_______}\\,,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={cos}^{2}⁡y$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$ is given above. What is the $2$nd  component of the product?",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that $x={cos}^{2}⁡y\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={cos}^{2}⁡y,$ and  \n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f={cos}^{2}⁡y,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y{cos}^{3}⁡y,\\,{cos}^{2}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
